<div id="alg1.l17" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l17.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">17:</span></span>
              
              
              Each agent <math id="alg1.l17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> estimates policy gradient:

<table id="S4.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{g}_{i}(k)\leftarrow\sum_{t=0}^{T}\gamma^{t}\cdot\frac{1}{n}\sum_{j\in\mathcal{N}_{i}^{\kappa}}\hat{Q}_{j}^{T}(\mathbf{s}^{\circ}_{\mathcal{N}_{j}^{\kappa}}(t),\mathbf{a}_{\mathcal{N}_{j}^{\kappa}}(t),\hat{\boldsymbol{\omega}}^{\circ}_{\mathcal{N}_{j}^{\kappa}})\nabla_{\theta_{i}}\log\pi_{i}^{\theta_{i}(k)}(\mathbf{a}_{i}(t)\mid\mathbf{s}^{\circ}_{\mathcal{N}_{i}}(t),\hat{\boldsymbol{\omega}}^{\circ}_{\mathcal{N}_{i}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">←</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>κ</mi></msubsup></mrow></munder><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐬</mi><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>κ</mi></msubsup><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>κ</mi></msubsup></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝎</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>κ</mi></msubsup><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mi>log</mi><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐬</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝎</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{g}_{i}(k)\leftarrow\sum_{t=0}^{T}\gamma^{t}\cdot\frac{1}{n}\sum_{j\in\mathcal{N}_{i}^{\kappa}}\hat{Q}_{j}^{T}(\mathbf{s}^{\circ}_{\mathcal{N}_{j}^{\kappa}}(t),\mathbf{a}_{\mathcal{N}_{j}^{\kappa}}(t),\hat{\boldsymbol{\omega}}^{\circ}_{\mathcal{N}_{j}^{\kappa}})\nabla_{\theta_{i}}\log\pi_{i}^{\theta_{i}(k)}(\mathbf{a}_{i}(t)\mid\mathbf{s}^{\circ}_{\mathcal{N}_{i}}(t),\hat{\boldsymbol{\omega}}^{\circ}_{\mathcal{N}_{i}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l18" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l18.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">18:</span></span>
              
              
              Update policy: <math id="alg1.l18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{i}(k+1)\leftarrow\theta_{i}(k)+\eta_{k}\hat{g}_{i}(k)\ \text{with stepsize }\eta_{k}\leftarrow\frac{\eta}{\sqrt{k+1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>with stepsize </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mfrac><mi>η</mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{i}(k+1)\leftarrow\theta_{i}(k)+\eta_{k}\hat{g}_{i}(k)\ \text{with stepsize }\eta_{k}\leftarrow\frac{\eta}{\sqrt{k+1}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l19" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l19.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">19:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l19.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l19.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l20" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l20.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">20:</span></span>
              
              
            Collect few trajectories <math id="alg1.l20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(\mathbf{s}(t),\mathbf{a}(t),\mathbf{s}(t+1))\}_{t=0}^{T_{a}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(\mathbf{s}(t),\mathbf{a}(t),\mathbf{s}(t+1))\}_{t=0}^{T_{a}}</annotation></semantics></math> in the new domain <span id="alg1.l20.3" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg1.l20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l20.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">P4: generalization</span>
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l21" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l21.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">21:</span></span>
              
              
            Each agent <math id="alg1.l21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> estimates new domain factor <math id="alg1.l21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\boldsymbol{\omega}}_{i}^{M+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝎</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\boldsymbol{\omega}}_{i}^{M+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and deploy policy <math id="alg1.l21.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi^{\theta(K)}_{i}(\cdot|\mathbf{s}^{\circ}_{\mathcal{N}_{i}},\hat{\boldsymbol{\omega}}^{M+1}_{\mathcal{N}_{i}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐬</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝎</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\theta(K)}_{i}(\cdot|\mathbf{s}^{\circ}_{\mathcal{N}_{i}},\hat{\boldsymbol{\omega}}^{M+1}_{\mathcal{N}_{i}})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Causal recovery and domain factor estimation</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we first discuss the computational overhead of causal discovery and ACR construction, and then provide the theoretical guarantees for causal recovery and domain factor estimation in Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (Structural identifiability in networked MARL). ‣ 5 Causal recovery and domain factor estimation ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and Proposition <a href="#Thmproposition4" title="Proposition 4 (Informal). ‣ 5 Causal recovery and domain factor estimation ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>-<a href="#Thmproposition5" title="Proposition 5 (Informal). ‣ 5 Causal recovery and domain factor estimation ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. Phase 1 (causal discovery) and Phase 2 (ACR construction) introduce upfront costs that are only one-time, local, and amortized. In particular, they are one-time preprocessing steps for each source domain and do not need to be repeated during meta-training or adaptation. Both steps are local per agent and over small neighborhoods, parallel across agents, and the results are re-used for the entire meta-training horizon and for adaptation.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Structural identifiability in networked MARL)<span id="Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Under the standard faithfulness assumption, the structural matrices <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{c}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{c}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#S2.E6" title="In Networked MARL under domain generalization ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> are identifiable from the observed data.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (Structural identifiability in networked MARL). ‣ 5 Causal recovery and domain factor estimation ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> guarantees that the underlying structure, that encodes how neighboring states, local actions, and latent domain factors affect local transitions, can be uniquely recovered from trajectories under standard causal discovery assumptions. The proof is deferred to Appendix <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Missing proofs in Section ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>. Furthermore, Proposition <a href="#Thmproposition4" title="Proposition 4 (Informal). ‣ 5 Causal recovery and domain factor estimation ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> provides a finite-sample guarantee for recovering the local structural dependencies.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproposition4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4</span></span><span id="Thmproposition4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Informal)<span id="Thmproposition4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproposition4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Under standard assumptions, including faithfulness, minimum mutual information for true causal links, bounded in-degree <math id="Thmproposition4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math>, sub-Gaussian noise, and Lipschitz continuity of the transition function, the sample complexity to recover the causal masks satisfies</p>
<table id="S5.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{\dim(\mathbf{s}_{\mathcal{N}_{i}})\cdot d_{\max}\log(\dim(\mathbf{s}_{\mathcal{N}_{i}})\cdot n/\delta)}{\lambda^{2}}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{\dim(\mathbf{s}_{\mathcal{N}_{i}})\cdot d_{\max}\log(\dim(\mathbf{s}_{\mathcal{N}_{i}})\cdot n/\delta)}{\lambda^{2}}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmproposition4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with probability at least <math id="Thmproposition4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Thmproposition4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> quantifies signal strength.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The required sample size scales almost linearly with the number of observed variables <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(\mathbf{s}_{\mathcal{N}_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(\mathbf{s}_{\mathcal{N}_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the sparsity level <math id="S5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math>, and decays quadratically with the strength of causal signals <math id="S5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. The formal statement and proof of Proposition <a href="#Thmproposition4" title="Proposition 4 (Informal). ‣ 5 Causal recovery and domain factor estimation ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, and discussions on the imposed assumptions are deferred to Appendix <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Missing proofs in Section ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproposition5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 5</span></span><span id="Thmproposition5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Informal)<span id="Thmproposition5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproposition5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose the causal masks are recovered and the domain-dependent transition dynamics are identifiable. Assume that distinct domain factors induce distinguishable state transitions in total variation, and that <math id="Thmproposition5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is compact with diameter <math id="Thmproposition5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\Omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\Omega}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, with probability at least <math id="Thmproposition5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, the estimated factor <math id="Thmproposition5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\boldsymbol{\omega}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝎</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\boldsymbol{\omega}}</annotation></semantics></math> given a trajectory of length <math id="Thmproposition5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{e}</annotation></semantics></math> generated from true factor <math id="Thmproposition5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\omega}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝝎</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\omega}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies</p>
<table id="S5.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\hat{\boldsymbol{\omega}}-\boldsymbol{\omega}^{*}\|_{2}\leq\delta_{\omega}(T_{e})=\mathcal{O}\left(\sqrt{\frac{D_{\Omega}\log(nT_{e}/\delta)}{T_{e}}}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝎</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>𝝎</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>ω</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mfrac></msqrt><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\hat{\boldsymbol{\omega}}-\boldsymbol{\omega}^{*}\|_{2}\leq\delta_{\omega}(T_{e})=\mathcal{O}\left(\sqrt{\frac{D_{\Omega}\log(nT_{e}/\delta)}{T_{e}}}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The estimation error decays as <math id="S5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1/\sqrt{T_{e}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1/\sqrt{T_{e}})</annotation></semantics></math> with high probability, and depends logarithmically on the number of agents. The result highlights that domain generalization can be performed efficiently with only a few samples. The formal statement and proof of Proposition <a href="#Thmproposition5" title="Proposition 5 (Informal). ‣ 5 Causal recovery and domain factor estimation ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> are deferred to Appendix <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Missing proofs in Section ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Convergence results and adaptation gap</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For clarity of analysis, we first establish convergence and adaptation guarantees for an <em id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">ACR-free</em> variant of <span id="S6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GSAC</span> (Algorithm <a href="#alg5" title="Algorithm 5 ‣ D.4 ACR-free GSAC ‣ Appendix D Algorithm design ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, detailed in Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Algorithm design ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>). Theoretical results for <span id="S6.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GSAC</span> with ACR follow as corollaries. We define the expected return of a policy parameterized by <math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> using an estimated domain factor <math id="S6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\omega}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝝎</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\omega}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in a true environment <math id="S6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝎</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\omega}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S6.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="J(\theta,\boldsymbol{\omega}^{\prime};\boldsymbol{\omega}):=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{s}\sim\rho_{0}}\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{a}(t)\sim\pi^{\theta}(\cdot|\mathbf{s}(t),\boldsymbol{\omega}^{\prime}),\mathbf{s}(t+1)\sim P_{\boldsymbol{\omega}}(\mathbf{s}(t),\mathbf{a}(t))}\left[\sum_{t=0}^{\infty}\gamma^{t}r(\mathbf{s}(t),\mathbf{a}(t))\Bigg|\mathbf{s}(0)=\mathbf{s}\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝝎</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>;</mo><mi>𝝎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝎</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝝎</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(\theta,\boldsymbol{\omega}^{\prime};\boldsymbol{\omega}):=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{s}\sim\rho_{0}}\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{a}(t)\sim\pi^{\theta}(\cdot|\mathbf{s}(t),\boldsymbol{\omega}^{\prime}),\mathbf{s}(t+1)\sim P_{\boldsymbol{\omega}}(\mathbf{s}(t),\mathbf{a}(t))}\left[\sum_{t=0}^{\infty}\gamma^{t}r(\mathbf{s}(t),\mathbf{a}(t))\Bigg|\mathbf{s}(0)=\mathbf{s}\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\theta}(\mathbf{a}|\mathbf{s},\boldsymbol{\omega})=\prod_{i=1}^{n}\pi_{i}^{\theta_{i}}(\mathbf{a}_{i}|\mathbf{s}_{\mathcal{N}_{i}},\boldsymbol{\omega}_{\mathcal{N}_{i}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝎</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝝎</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\theta}(\mathbf{a}|\mathbf{s},\boldsymbol{\omega})=\prod_{i=1}^{n}\pi_{i}^{\theta_{i}}(\mathbf{a}_{i}|\mathbf{s}_{\mathcal{N}_{i}},\boldsymbol{\omega}_{\mathcal{N}_{i}})</annotation></semantics></math> is the joint domain-conditioned policy. For notational simplicity, we write <math id="S6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(\theta,\boldsymbol{\omega}):=J(\theta,\boldsymbol{\omega};\boldsymbol{\omega})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝎</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(\theta,\boldsymbol{\omega}):=J(\theta,\boldsymbol{\omega};\boldsymbol{\omega})</annotation></semantics></math> when the estimated and true domain factors coincide. To this end, for domain generalization our objective under domain distribution <math id="S6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> is:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{\theta\in\Theta}J(\theta):=\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{\omega}\sim\mathcal{D}}[J(\theta,\boldsymbol{\omega})]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝝎</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{\theta\in\Theta}J(\theta):=\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{\omega}\sim\mathcal{D}}[J(\theta,\boldsymbol{\omega})].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1 </span>Convergence</h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We begin by introducing standard assumptions (Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1. ‣ 6.1 Convergence ‣ 6 Convergence results and adaptation gap ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>-<a href="#Thmassumption4" title="Assumption 4. ‣ 6.1 Convergence ‣ 6 Convergence results and adaptation gap ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) used in networked MARL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>)</cite>, as well as additional ones (Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption5" title="Assumption 5. ‣ 6.1 Convergence ‣ 6 Convergence results and adaptation gap ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>-<a href="#Thmassumption6" title="Assumption 6. ‣ 6.1 Convergence ‣ 6 Convergence results and adaptation gap ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) for the domain generalization setting.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 1</span></span><span id="Thmassumption1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Rewards are bounded: <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq r_{i}(\mathbf{s}_{i},\mathbf{a}_{i})\leq\bar{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq r_{i}(\mathbf{s}_{i},\mathbf{a}_{i})\leq\bar{r}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,\mathbf{s}_{i},\mathbf{a}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,\mathbf{s}_{i},\mathbf{a}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. The local state and action spaces satisfy <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{S}_{i}|\leq S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{S}_{i}|\leq S</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{A}_{i}|\leq A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{A}_{i}|\leq A</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 2</span></span><span id="Thmassumption2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There exist <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> such that, for any <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝎</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\omega}</annotation></semantics></math>, the local transition probabilities satisfy <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}_{\boldsymbol{\omega}}\left((\mathbf{s}_{\mathcal{N}_{i}^{\kappa}}(\tau),\mathbf{a}_{\mathcal{N}_{i}^{\kappa}}(\tau))=(\mathbf{s}^{\prime},\mathbf{a}^{\prime})\mid(\mathbf{s}(1),\mathbf{a}(1))=(\mathbf{s},\mathbf{a})\right)\geq\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>𝝎</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>κ</mi></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>κ</mi></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}_{\boldsymbol{\omega}}\left((\mathbf{s}_{\mathcal{N}_{i}^{\kappa}}(\tau),\mathbf{a}_{\mathcal{N}_{i}^{\kappa}}(\tau))=(\mathbf{s}^{\prime},\mathbf{a}^{\prime})\mid(\mathbf{s}(1),\mathbf{a}(1))=(\mathbf{s},\mathbf{a})\right)\geq\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>
for all <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and all <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((\mathbf{s}^{\prime},\mathbf{a}^{\prime}),(\mathbf{s},\mathbf{a}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((\mathbf{s}^{\prime},\mathbf{a}^{\prime}),(\mathbf{s},\mathbf{a}))</annotation></semantics></math> in the appropriate product spaces.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 3</span></span><span id="Thmassumption3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For all <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}_{\mathcal{N}_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}_{\mathcal{N}_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\omega}_{\mathcal{N}_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝎</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\omega}_{\mathcal{N}_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{a}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{a}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\nabla_{\theta_{i}}\log\pi_{i}^{\theta_{i}}(\mathbf{a}_{i}|\mathbf{s}_{\mathcal{N}_{i}},\boldsymbol{\omega}_{\mathcal{N}_{i}})\|\leq L_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝝎</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\nabla_{\theta_{i}}\log\pi_{i}^{\theta_{i}}(\mathbf{a}_{i}|\mathbf{s}_{\mathcal{N}_{i}},\boldsymbol{\omega}_{\mathcal{N}_{i}})\|\leq L_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi^{\theta}(\mathbf{a}|\mathbf{s},\boldsymbol{\omega})\|\leq L:=\sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^{n}L_{i}^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝎</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>:=</mo><msqrt><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi^{\theta}(\mathbf{a}|\mathbf{s},\boldsymbol{\omega})\|\leq L:=\sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^{n}L_{i}^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla J(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla J(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz continuous in <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 4</span></span><span id="Thmassumption4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each agent’s parameter space <math id="Thmassumption4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{i}\subset\mathbb{R}^{d_{i}^{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{i}\subset\mathbb{R}^{d_{i}^{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> is compact with diameter bounded by <math id="Thmassumption4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\Theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\Theta}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 5</span></span><span id="Thmassumption5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For all <math id="Thmassumption5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>: (i) <math id="Thmassumption5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}(\mathbf{s}_{i}(t+1)|\mathbf{s}_{\mathcal{N}_{i}}(t),\mathbf{a}_{i}(t),\boldsymbol{\omega}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝝎</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}(\mathbf{s}_{i}(t+1)|\mathbf{s}_{\mathcal{N}_{i}}(t),\mathbf{a}_{i}(t),\boldsymbol{\omega}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmassumption5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{P}</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz in <math id="Thmassumption5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\omega}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝎</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\omega}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>; (ii) <math id="Thmassumption5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{i}^{\theta}(\mathbf{s},\mathbf{a},\boldsymbol{\omega})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{i}^{\theta}(\mathbf{s},\mathbf{a},\boldsymbol{\omega})</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmassumption5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz in <math id="Thmassumption5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝎</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\omega}</annotation></semantics></math>; (iii) <math id="Thmassumption5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\theta_{i}}J(\theta,\boldsymbol{\omega})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\theta_{i}}J(\theta,\boldsymbol{\omega})</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmassumption5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{J}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{J}</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz in <math id="Thmassumption5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝎</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\omega}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 6</span></span><span id="Thmassumption6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The domain factor space <math id="Thmassumption6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is compact with diameter bounded by <math id="Thmassumption6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\Omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\Omega}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Discussion on assumptions.</h5>

<div id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1. ‣ 6.1 Convergence ‣ 6 Convergence results and adaptation gap ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>-<a href="#Thmassumption4" title="Assumption 4. ‣ 6.1 Convergence ‣ 6 Convergence results and adaptation gap ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> are standard for proving convergence of networked MARL algorithms, without consideration of domain generalization/adaptation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. To account for generalizability across domains in networked systems, we introduce additional Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption5" title="Assumption 5. ‣ 6.1 Convergence ‣ 6 Convergence results and adaptation gap ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>-<a href="#Thmassumption6" title="Assumption 6. ‣ 6.1 Convergence ‣ 6 Convergence results and adaptation gap ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> regarding the latent domain factor. Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption5" title="Assumption 5. ‣ 6.1 Convergence ‣ 6 Convergence results and adaptation gap ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> is similar with Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption3" title="Assumption 3. ‣ 6.1 Convergence ‣ 6 Convergence results and adaptation gap ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, which imposes the smoothness w.r.t. the domain factor, while Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption3" title="Assumption 3. ‣ 6.1 Convergence ‣ 6 Convergence results and adaptation gap ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> imposes the smoothness w.r.t. the actor parameter <math id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>. Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption6" title="Assumption 6. ‣ 6.1 Convergence ‣ 6 Convergence results and adaptation gap ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> is a regularity assumption to ensure the compactness of domain factor space. We provide a detailed discussion on these assumption in Appendix <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Missing proofs in Section ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Critic error bound.</h5>

<div id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first analyze the inner-loop critic update. Fixing any outer iteration <math id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, and omitting <math id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> from the notation, we establish the following result. Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2 (Critic error bound, informal). ‣ Critic error bound. ‣ 6.1 Convergence ‣ 6 Convergence results and adaptation gap ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows that the inner loop converges to an estimate of <math id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with steady-state error decaying with <math id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\sqrt{T_{e}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\sqrt{T_{e}}</annotation></semantics></math> and exponentially in <math id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>. The proof and formal statement of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2 (Critic error bound, informal). ‣ Critic error bound. ‣ 6.1 Convergence ‣ 6 Convergence results and adaptation gap ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> are deferred to Appendix <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Missing proofs in Section ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Critic error bound, informal)<span id="Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Under Assumptions <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1. ‣ 6.1 Convergence ‣ 6 Convergence results and adaptation gap ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>–<a href="#Thmassumption6" title="Assumption 6. ‣ 6.1 Convergence ‣ 6 Convergence results and adaptation gap ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, and for any <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, if the critic stepsize is set as <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}=h/(t+t_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}=h/(t+t_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> and the domain factor <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\boldsymbol{\omega}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝎</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\boldsymbol{\omega}}</annotation></semantics></math> is estimated from <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{e}</annotation></semantics></math> trajectories, then with probability at least <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, the critic estimate satisfies:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|Q_{i}(\mathbf{s},\mathbf{a},\boldsymbol{\omega})-\hat{Q}_{i}^{T}(\mathbf{s}_{\mathcal{N}_{i}^{\kappa}},\mathbf{a}_{\mathcal{N}_{i}^{\kappa}},\hat{\boldsymbol{\omega}}_{\mathcal{N}_{i}^{\kappa}})|\leq\frac{C_{a}}{\sqrt{T+t_{0}}}+\frac{C^{\prime}_{a}}{T+t_{0}}+\frac{2c\rho^{\kappa+1}}{(1-\gamma)^{2}}+C^{\prime}_{\boldsymbol{\omega}}\sqrt{\frac{\log(nT_{e}/\delta)}{T_{e}}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>κ</mi></msubsup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>κ</mi></msubsup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝎</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>κ</mi></msubsup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><msqrt><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msqrt></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>𝝎</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mfrac></msqrt></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|Q_{i}(\mathbf{s},\mathbf{a},\boldsymbol{\omega})-\hat{Q}_{i}^{T}(\mathbf{s}_{\mathcal{N}_{i}^{\kappa}},\mathbf{a}_{\mathcal{N}_{i}^{\kappa}},\hat{\boldsymbol{\omega}}_{\mathcal{N}_{i}^{\kappa}})|\leq\frac{C_{a}}{\sqrt{T+t_{0}}}+\frac{C^{\prime}_{a}}{T+t_{0}}+\frac{2c\rho^{\kappa+1}}{(1-\gamma)^{2}}+C^{\prime}_{\boldsymbol{\omega}}\sqrt{\frac{\log(nT_{e}/\delta)}{T_{e}}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{a}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\prime}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\prime}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\boldsymbol{\omega}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>𝝎</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\boldsymbol{\omega}}</annotation></semantics></math> are constants. These will be further characterized and discussed in Section <a href="#S6.SS3" title="6.3 Additional gains from ACR ‣ 6 Convergence results and adaptation gap ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a>, where we analyze the additional benefits of incorporating ACR.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Actor convergence.</h5>

<div id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Based on the critic bound, we derive the bound on policy gradient updates. The first term, of order <math id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(1/\sqrt{K+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(1/\sqrt{K+1})</annotation></semantics></math>, vanishes as the number of outer iterations <math id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> increases. The remaining three terms correspond to different sources of error: the second arises from neighborhood truncation and decays exponentially with <math id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>; the third stems from estimation of the domain factor <math id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝎</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\omega}</annotation></semantics></math>, with error decreasing as <math id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\sqrt{T_{e}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\sqrt{T_{e}}</annotation></semantics></math>; and the fourth reflects the approximation of the domain distribution <math id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> using only <math id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> sampled source domains, decaying with <math id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\sqrt{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>M</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\sqrt{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. The formal statement and proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3 (Policy gradient convergence, informal). ‣ Actor convergence. ‣ 6.1 Convergence ‣ 6 Convergence results and adaptation gap ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> are deferred to Appendix <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Missing proofs in Section ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Policy gradient convergence, informal)<span id="Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Under Assumptions <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1. ‣ 6.1 Convergence ‣ 6 Convergence results and adaptation gap ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>–<a href="#Thmassumption6" title="Assumption 6. ‣ 6.1 Convergence ‣ 6 Convergence results and adaptation gap ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, for <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> and sufficiently large <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, if the actor and critic stepsizes are chosen appropriately and domain factors are estimated from <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{e}</annotation></semantics></math> samples, then with probability at least <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\delta</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\sum_{k=0}^{K-1}\eta_{k}\|\nabla J(\theta(k))\|^{2}}{\sum_{k=0}^{K-1}\eta_{k}}\leq\tilde{\mathcal{O}}\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{K+1}}+\rho^{\kappa+1}+\sqrt{\frac{1}{T_{e}}}+\sqrt{\frac{1}{M}}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt></mfrac><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mfrac></msqrt><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>M</mi></mfrac></msqrt></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\sum_{k=0}^{K-1}\eta_{k}\|\nabla J(\theta(k))\|^{2}}{\sum_{k=0}^{K-1}\eta_{k}}\leq\tilde{\mathcal{O}}\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{K+1}}+\rho^{\kappa+1}+\sqrt{\frac{1}{T_{e}}}+\sqrt{\frac{1}{M}}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2 </span>Generalization</h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <span id="S6.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Phase 4</span>, for a new domain <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\omega}^{M+1}\sim\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝝎</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\omega}^{M+1}\sim\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="S6.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ACR-free GSAC</span> collects <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> trajectories, estimates <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\boldsymbol{\omega}}^{M+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝎</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\boldsymbol{\omega}}^{M+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and deploys the policy <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi^{\theta(K)}(\cdot|\mathbf{s},\hat{\boldsymbol{\omega}}^{M+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝎</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\theta(K)}(\cdot|\mathbf{s},\hat{\boldsymbol{\omega}}^{M+1})</annotation></semantics></math>. The expected return is:</p>
<table id="S6.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle J(\theta(K),\hat{\boldsymbol{\omega}}^{M+1};\boldsymbol{\omega}^{M+1})=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{s}(0)\sim\rho_{0}}\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{a}(t)\sim\pi^{\theta}(\cdot|\mathbf{s}(t),\hat{\boldsymbol{\omega}}^{M+1}),\mathbf{s}(t+1)\sim P_{\boldsymbol{\omega}^{M+1}}(\mathbf{s}(t),\mathbf{a}(t))}\left[\sum_{t=0}^{\infty}\gamma^{t}r_{t}\right]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝎</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>𝝎</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝎</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>𝝎</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover></mstyle><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle J(\theta(K),\hat{\boldsymbol{\omega}}^{M+1};\boldsymbol{\omega}^{M+1})=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{s}(0)\sim\rho_{0}}\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{a}(t)\sim\pi^{\theta}(\cdot|\mathbf{s}(t),\hat{\boldsymbol{\omega}}^{M+1}),\mathbf{s}(t+1)\sim P_{\boldsymbol{\omega}^{M+1}}(\mathbf{s}(t),\mathbf{a}(t))}\left[\sum_{t=0}^{\infty}\gamma^{t}r_{t}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Adaptation guarantee)<span id="Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Under Assumptions <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1. ‣ 6.1 Convergence ‣ 6 Convergence results and adaptation gap ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>–<a href="#Thmassumption6" title="Assumption 6. ‣ 6.1 Convergence ‣ 6 Convergence results and adaptation gap ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, with probability at least <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\delta</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\left[J(\theta(K),\hat{\boldsymbol{\omega}}^{M+1};\boldsymbol{\omega}^{M+1})\mid\theta(K)\right]\geq J(\theta(K))-L_{\boldsymbol{\omega}^{\prime}}C_{\boldsymbol{\omega}}\sqrt{\frac{\log(n/\delta)}{T_{a}}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝎</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝝎</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><msup><mi>𝝎</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>𝝎</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mfrac></msqrt></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\left[J(\theta(K),\hat{\boldsymbol{\omega}}^{M+1};\boldsymbol{\omega}^{M+1})\mid\theta(K)\right]\geq J(\theta(K))-L_{\boldsymbol{\omega}^{\prime}}C_{\boldsymbol{\omega}}\sqrt{\frac{\log(n/\delta)}{T_{a}}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4 (Adaptation guarantee). ‣ 6.2 Generalization ‣ 6 Convergence results and adaptation gap ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> establishes that the adaptation gap decreases at a rate of <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(1/\sqrt{T_{a}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(1/\sqrt{T_{a}})</annotation></semantics></math>, relative to the return of the meta-trained policy. The proof is deferred to Appendix <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Missing proofs in Section ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.3 </span>Additional gains from ACR</h3>

<div id="S6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Notably, ACR introduces only a constant-factor increase in approximation error over truncation (multiplicative factor 3), and thus all ACR-free convergence and generalization results naturally extend to <span id="S6.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GSAC</span>. Beyond computational gains discussed in Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Approximately compact representation ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, ACR also improves <em id="S6.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">sample efficiency</em>. In Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2 (Critic error bound, informal). ‣ Critic error bound. ‣ 6.1 Convergence ‣ 6 Convergence results and adaptation gap ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the explicit constants are defined as <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{a}:=\frac{6\bar{\epsilon}}{1-\sqrt{\gamma}}\sqrt{\frac{\tau h}{\sigma}[\log(\frac{2\tau T^{2}}{\delta})+f(\kappa)\log SA]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mi>γ</mi></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mi>σ</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mi>δ</mi></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{a}:=\frac{6\bar{\epsilon}}{1-\sqrt{\gamma}}\sqrt{\frac{\tau h}{\sigma}[\log(\frac{2\tau T^{2}}{\delta})+f(\kappa)\log SA]}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\epsilon}:=4\frac{\bar{r}}{1-\gamma}+2\bar{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\epsilon}:=4\frac{\bar{r}}{1-\gamma}+2\bar{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\prime}_{a}:=\frac{2}{1-\sqrt{\gamma}}\max(\frac{16\bar{\epsilon}h\tau}{\sigma},\frac{2\bar{r}}{1-\gamma}(\tau+t_{0}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mi>γ</mi></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mi>σ</mi></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\prime}_{a}:=\frac{2}{1-\sqrt{\gamma}}\max(\frac{16\bar{\epsilon}h\tau}{\sigma},\frac{2\bar{r}}{1-\gamma}(\tau+t_{0}))</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\prime}_{\boldsymbol{\omega}}:=L_{Q}C_{\boldsymbol{\omega}}\sqrt{D_{\Omega}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>𝝎</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>𝝎</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>D</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\prime}_{\boldsymbol{\omega}}:=L_{Q}C_{\boldsymbol{\omega}}\sqrt{D_{\Omega}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the stepsize <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}=\frac{h}{t+t_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}=\frac{h}{t+t_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\geq\frac{1}{\sigma}\max(2,\frac{1}{1-\sqrt{\gamma}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>σ</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mi>γ</mi></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\geq\frac{1}{\sigma}\max(2,\frac{1}{1-\sqrt{\gamma}})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{0}\geq\max(2h,4\sigma h,\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{0}\geq\max(2h,4\sigma h,\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>. By leveraging ACR, the effective dimensionality of the <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>-hop state space is dramatically reduced, leading to smaller <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> and larger <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> in Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption2" title="Assumption 2. ‣ 6.1 Convergence ‣ 6 Convergence results and adaptation gap ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. This reduces <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{a}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\prime}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\prime}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, enabling faster convergence and reducing the required inner-loop iterations <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> to achieve a given accuracy in <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>-value estimation of within critic, thereby decreasing the total sample complexity.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Numerical experiments</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate <span id="S7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GSAC</span> on two standard benchmarks for networked MARL algorithms: wireless communications <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>)</cite> and traffic control <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>)</cite> (the latter deferred to Appendix <a href="#A8.SS2" title="H.2 Traffic control ‣ Appendix H Comprehensive Experiments ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H.2</span></a>). A detailed description of the experimental setup and comprehensive results are provided in Appendix <a href="#A8" title="Appendix H Comprehensive Experiments ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H</span></a>. We consider <math id="S7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=3</annotation></semantics></math> source domains, each with domain factors <math id="S7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\omega}\in\{0.2,0.5,0.8\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝎</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0.2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\omega}\in\{0.2,0.5,0.8\}</annotation></semantics></math>, while the target domain uses <math id="S7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\omega}_{\text{target}}=0.65" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝝎</mi><mtext>target</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.65</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\omega}_{\text{target}}=0.65</annotation></semantics></math> unless otherwise specified. In each domain, <span id="S7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GSAC</span> is trained for <math id="S7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> outer iterations (depending on convergence), with a inner loop horizon <math id="S7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=10</annotation></semantics></math>. For domain factor estimation, we collect <math id="S7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{e}=20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{e}=20</annotation></semantics></math> trajectories per domain. To evaluate adaptation performance in the target domain, we compare <span id="S7.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GSAC</span> against three baselines:</p>
<ul id="S7.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S7.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">GSAC<span id="S7.I1.i1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> (ours)</span></span>: Learn <math id="S7.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}(a|s,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}(a|s,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> by optimizing <math id="S7.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> over source domains.
At test time, estimate <math id="S7.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and directly deploy <math id="S7.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}(a|s,\omega^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}(a|s,\omega^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> in the target domain without further training.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">SAC-MTL<span id="S7.I1.i2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> (multi-task)</span></span>: Learn <math id="S7.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}(a|s,z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}(a|s,z)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S7.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <em id="S7.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">pre-specified</em> one-hot encoding of each source domain, and jointly optimize <math id="S7.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> across domains <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>)</cite>; deploy <math id="S7.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}(a|s,z^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}(a|s,z^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> in the target domain.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">SAC-FT<span id="S7.I1.i3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> (fine-tune)</span></span>: Train a single policy <math id="S7.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}(a|s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}(a|s)</annotation></semantics></math> across source domains without domain-factor conditioning and fine-tune <math id="S7.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> in the target domain.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">SAC-LFS<span id="S7.I1.i4.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> (learning from scratch)</span></span>: Train <math id="S7.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}(a|s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}(a|s)</annotation></semantics></math> in the target domain without prior meta-training.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate both scalability and generalizability, we vary the grid size of the wireless communication network (<math id="S7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{grid size}\in\{3,4,5\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>grid size</mtext><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{grid size}\in\{3,4,5\}</annotation></semantics></math>), which affects both the number of users and the connectivity structure (see Figure <a href="#A8.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Domain shift sensitivity. ‣ H.1 Wireless communication ‣ Appendix H Comprehensive Experiments ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>-<a href="#A8.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Domain shift sensitivity. ‣ H.1 Wireless communication ‣ Appendix H Comprehensive Experiments ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>). As shown in Figure <a href="#S7.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 7 Numerical experiments ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and Figure <a href="#A8.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ Domain shift sensitivity. ‣ H.1 Wireless communication ‣ Appendix H Comprehensive Experiments ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, <span id="S7.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GSAC</span> consistently maintains high training and adaptation performance across grid sizes, demonstrating its scalability and generalizability. In particular, <span id="S7.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GSAC</span> achieves the best few-shot performance (Episodes 1–30), reflecting rapid adaptation from minimal target data.
In contrast, <span id="S7.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">SAC-MTL</span> exhibits moderate performance during the early adaptation phase but improves steadily over time. This is because multi-task learning leverages shared structure across source domains, yet lacks explicit domain-factor conditioning, leading to slower adaptation compared to <span id="S7.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GSAC</span>. <span id="S7.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">SAC-FT</span> performs worse in the early phase due to its need to fine-tune policy parameters directly in the target domain, resulting in higher sample complexity. <span id="S7.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">SAC-LFS</span> suffers the slowest convergence and lowest overall return, underscoring the importance of meta-training and domain factor conditioning for efficient cross-domain adaptation.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S7.F2.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.21427v1/figures/p3_GS3_TP0.65.png" id="S7.F2.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/286;" width="476" height="286" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S7.F2.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S7.F2.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">grid size 3 (16 agents)</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S7.F2.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.21427v1/figures/p3_GS4_TP0.65.png" id="S7.F2.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/286;" width="476" height="286" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S7.F2.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S7.F2.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">grid size 4 (25 agents)</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S7.F2.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.21427v1/figures/p3_GS5_TP0.65.png" id="S7.F2.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/286;" width="476" height="286" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S7.F2.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="S7.F2.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">grid size 5 (36 agents)</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S7.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S7.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Adaptation comparison for different grid sizes in wireless communication benchmarks.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8 </span>Conclusions and future work</h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We presented <span id="S8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GSAC</span>, a causality-aware MARL framework that integrates ACR construction with meta actor–critic learning, achieving provable scalability, fast adaptation, and strong generalization across domains. We established quasi-linear sample complexity and finite-sample convergence guarantees, and showed empirically that <span id="S8.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GSAC</span> consistently outperforms competitive baselines on challenging networked MARL benchmarks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The main limitation of our current work is that <span id="S8.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GSAC</span> has only been evaluated on tabular and fully observed benchmarks. Nonetheless, it establishes a principled foundation for practical control of large-scale networked systems. Promising future directions include:
(i) extending <span id="S8.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GSAC</span> to continuous state and action spaces with function approximation based on ACRs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>;
(ii) broadening empirical evaluation to more diverse and large-scale networked systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>; and
(iii) incorporating partial observability into the framework.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Acknowledgments</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We would like to thank all the anonymous reviewers for their careful proofreading and constructive feedback, which have greatly improved the quality of this work. In particular, we thank one reviewer for the insightful suggestions on adding the roadmap linking theorems and algorithms in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Generalizable and scalable actor-critic ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, as well as for recommending additional discussions on Assumptions <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1. ‣ 6.1 Convergence ‣ 6 Convergence results and adaptation gap ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>–<a href="#Thmassumption6" title="Assumption 6. ‣ 6.1 Convergence ‣ 6 Convergence results and adaptation gap ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. We also thank the other reviewers for suggesting the inclusion of two additional baseline methods and an extra benchmark, along with further discussions on the assumptions underlying Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (Structural identifiability in networked MARL). ‣ 5 Causal recovery and domain factor estimation ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and Proposition <a href="#Thmproposition4" title="Proposition 4 (Informal). ‣ 5 Causal recovery and domain factor estimation ‣ Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. We are grateful to Chengdong Ma for valuable initial discussions. This work was supported by the Engineering and Physical Sciences Research Council (EPSRC) under grant numbers EP/Y003187/1 and UKRI849.</p>
</div>
</section>
